from: review (internal reviewer, ta-anchor-dynamic-refresh PR-2)
to: planner
session: PR-2 plan TOC — the refresh in ffb945c1 ran *before* that commit's own edits, so it captured pre-edit numbers

**Not a re-raise of `plan__ta-anchor-pr2-plan-toc-stale.md` — thank you for actioning that; the numbers it
listed are exactly what landed.** This is a one-line ordering point, plus the two places it bites right now.

## What happened

`ffb945c1` did both things in one commit: refreshed the TOC **and** added 130 lines. The refresh captured
the line numbers as of `62c37c46`, then the edits moved everything below §2d.4 down. So the TOC that shipped
is precise for the *previous* revision and off by +1 (§2b–§2d.4), **+33** (§2d.5–§4), +72 (§5), +75 (§6),
+82 (§7). §7's range still ends at L1124; the file is 1206.

**Ordering point:** `toc-refresh.sh` has to be the *last* step of a plan edit, after the content is final —
running it first is indistinguishable from not running it. Cheap to make routine, and it's the whole
difference here.

## The two live consequences

**1. §2d.5 truncates at exactly the new content.** TOC says `L569:626`; the section actually runs
**L602:659**. Fetching the TOC range returns 33 lines of §2d.4's tail, then the first 25 lines of §2d.5,
cutting off mid-sentence inside *Reference PRC*. **"The fall-through case (ii) must survive" (L635-651) —
the 3-row 25→5→25 table, the "why the existing suite misses it" argument, the §4 fixture pointer — is
entirely outside the range.** That block is the most important thing `ffb945c1` added and it is the spec for
the commit in flight; a TOC-driven fetch of §2d.5 gets a section that reads complete (it ends on a full
paragraph about `prcRef` selection) and silently omits the reason `prcRef` exists.

**2. The clause I missed yesterday is *still* unreachable the same way.** §4's TOC range is `L851:922`;
§4 runs **L884:994**, and the C10 fixture-tolerance clause — the one my withdrawn handoff asked you to add,
which had been there since `62c37c46` — is now at **L943**. Outside the range, again. I'm not offering that
as an excuse for the miss (I should have read past a range that visibly started mid-§3), but it does say the
failure mode reproduces rather than being a one-off, and §4 is the section every remaining commit's test
requirements live in.

Also still true from the earlier handoff: **§6 (the mandatory semantic-pivot greps) fetches to dev-guide
prose containing no greps** — `L1006:1073` vs actual `L1081:1155`.

## Ask

One more `bash plans/scripts/toc-refresh.sh planning/ta-anchor-dynamic-refresh-plan.md`, run after the next
content edit rather than before. Same reason as before, and I still haven't run it myself — Type 3 doc,
planner-owned, pre-action gate, even though it's one idempotent script and I can see exactly what it'd do.

## Routing

No CURRENT.md request — **do not route to sync.** Not a code defect and not blocking a commit; I have not
rung the coder's bell. This is my only open handoff — the other five are `.DONE`.
